<Article
  title="Gloomhaven Solo and Two-Player: Does It Work?"
  description="The campaign scales to any count, but the experience changes a lot. What to expect at each."
  game="gloomhaven"
  gameTitle="Gloomhaven"
  category="Guide"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Gloomhaven is cooperative, which means there's nothing stopping one person
    playing the whole thing. The scenarios scale properly to the number of
    characters, so the game is balanced at every count. The question is whether
    it's <em>good</em> at every count, and the answer varies more than the box
    suggests.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Two characters is the sweet spot — fast turns, real tactical depth, easy to schedule. Solo works well if you control two characters rather than one. Four is the most epic and by far the slowest." />

  <h2>How Scaling Works</h2>

  <p>
    Every scenario specifies monster counts and difficulty based on how many
    characters are in the party, and the recommended difficulty is derived from
    average character level. Nothing is left to judgement — a two-character
    scenario is designed as a two-character scenario, not a four-player one with
    monsters removed.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Characters, not players">
    The scaling counts characters on the board. One person running two characters
    plays exactly the same scenario as two people running one each. That's what
    makes solo play work so cleanly.
  </InfoBox>

  <ComparisonTable title="What each count feels like">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Count</th>
          <th>Session length</th>
          <th>Feel</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1 character</td>
          <td>Shortest</td>
          <td>Tight and fragile — no cover for mistakes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2 characters</td>
          <td>Fast</td>
          <td><strong>The sweet spot</strong> — real combos, quick turns</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3 characters</td>
          <td>Moderate</td>
          <td>Good variety, noticeably slower</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4 characters</td>
          <td>Longest</td>
          <td>Most epic, most downtime, hardest to schedule</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Playing Solo</h2>

  <p>
    Solo Gloomhaven is genuinely popular, and it works for a specific reason: the
    monsters are run by a deterministic behaviour deck, not by a human opponent.
    There's nothing to hide and nobody to referee, so playing alone loses none of
    the game's integrity.
  </p>

  <p>
    The one strong recommendation: <strong>run two characters, not one</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>One character is brutally fragile.</strong> With no one to tank, heal or reposition, a couple of bad modifier flips ends the scenario.</li>
    <li><strong>The card system needs a partner.</strong> Elements generated by one character and consumed by another is where the system sings, and a lone character can rarely do both.</li>
    <li><strong>Two hands is very manageable alone.</strong> You're already planning both halves of the turn — controlling two characters is less than twice the work, and you lose the secrecy rule that exists purely to stop players over-coordinating.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Solo makes the campaign layer better, not worse">
    Levelling, shopping, retiring characters and choosing story branches are all
    faster and more considered alone. Plenty of solo players get further into the
    campaign in six months than a four-player group manages in two years.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Two Players</h2>

  <p>
    Two is where Gloomhaven is at its best as a shared experience. Turns come
    round quickly, there's almost no downtime, and coordinating two characters is
    complex enough to be interesting without becoming a committee.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's also the most sustainable count in practice. Getting two people together
    regularly for a year is achievable; getting four together is a scheduling
    project — and campaigns die from scheduling far more often than from boredom.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Two characters means two card hands to track">
    With fewer characters, each one carries more responsibility and exhaustion
    hits harder. Losing one character at two players is close to losing the
    scenario, so plan rests earlier than you think you need to.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Three and Four Players</h2>

  <p>
    Four characters is the biggest, most dramatic version of Gloomhaven — bigger
    maps, more monsters, party compositions that let each class do exactly what
    it's designed for.
  </p>

  <p>
    It also has real downtime. While one player agonises over which two cards to
    commit, the other three wait, and the secrecy rule means they can't fully help
    without breaking it. Sessions stretch, and the difference between a
    two-character and four-character evening is closer to double than you'd like.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you play at four, the single most useful habit is choosing your two cards
    <em>while others are choosing theirs</em> rather than waiting your turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Which Should You Plan For?</h2>

  <p>
    Be honest about the group you actually have rather than the group you'd like.
    A two-player campaign that meets weekly will get further, faster, and be more
    fun than a four-player one that meets when everyone's free.
  </p>

  <p>
    And if you're buying for solo or two-player specifically, the case for
    starting with Jaws of the Lion is even stronger — smaller scenarios, faster
    setup, and a campaign length that one or two people can realistically finish.
    See <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-vs-jaws-of-the-lion/">which box to buy first</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
